      "id": "c1",
      "kind": "RULE",
      "text": "Input tax claims on motor car expenses for cars used by you or your employees are disallowed under Regulation 27 of the GST (General) Regulations, and this restriction applies regardless of whether the motor car is used wholly or partly for business purposes unless the car is excluded from the definition of a motor car under Regulation 25(1).",
      "supportIds": [
        "passage-1",
        "passage-4"
      ],
      "issueId": "issue_af07a4ab"
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "kind": "RULE",
      "text": "A motor car is defined as a motor car constructed or adapted for the carriage of not more than 7 passengers exclusive of the driver and with an unladen weight not exceeding 3,000 kilograms, subject to specific exclusions such as business service passenger vehicles registered before 1 April 1998, taxis, private cars for school transport, cars let on hire or sold by motor dealers or financial institutions under hire purchase, and cars used for instructional purposes for reward by a licensed driving school.",
      "supportIds": [
        "passage-2",
        "passage-5"
      ],
      "issueId": "issue_af07a4ab"
    },
    {
      "id": "c3",
      "kind": "RULE",
      "text": "From 1 January 2023, where a motor car is used by a connected person such as a subsidiary, GST incurred on motor car expenses is generally not claimable unless you recover the expenses from the connected person and the recovery is not ancillary to any primary supply made to them.",
      "supportIds": [
        "passage-3",
        "passage-6"
      ],
      "issueId": "issue_af07a4ab"
    },
    {
      "id": "c4",
      "kind": "APPLICATION",
      "text": "The input GST recovery eligibility for your company's private motor car remains unresolved because case-specific treatment, physical or economic use details, and whether the vehicle falls under any specific regulatory exclusions have not yet been fully established under IRAS rules.",
